+section('client-machines', 'Client machines: HW and SW', 'pc')
  p.
    Which machine you need depends on what the jobs run against. Clients are supported on
    #[strong Ubuntu 26.04]; other systemd-based Linux distributions work the same way in practice.
    Every Client machine needs outbound HTTPS to the Coordinator (and to wherever jobs download from:
    Artifactory, git servers, Docker registries). Nothing inbound.

  .row.g-3.mb-3
    .col-md-6
      .card.h-100.border-primary-subtle
        .card-header
          i.bi.bi-cpu.me-1
          strong HW Client: real machine with a physical DUT
        .card-body.small
          ul.mb-0
            li A physical machine (a mini-PC, NUC or Raspberry-class ARM64 board) next to the bench.
            li USB for the DUT's debugger (ST-Link), USB-serial adapters (UART) and DUT USB. A #[strong powered USB hub] is recommended.
            li #[code stlink-tools] and/or #[code openocd], #[code usbutils] (for the dashboard's USB scan).
            li Docker only if jobs run containers in their #[code --command].
            li One Client instance per board. One machine can drive several boards (up to 8 ST-Links, UARTs and USB devices per instance).
    .col-md-6
      .card.h-100.border-success-subtle
        .card-header
          i.bi.bi-pc-display.me-1
          strong SW Client: real or virtual machine, software-only jobs
        .card-body.small
          ul.mb-0
            li Any Linux host: bare metal, a VM (Proxmox, VMware, Hyper-V, KVM) or a cloud instance.
            li Whatever your jobs' commands use: git, Docker Engine (#[code docker.io]; install it before the Client so its service user gets the #[code docker] group), an emulator. The Client itself needs none of them.
            li Size the VM for one job plus your test tools: 4 vCPU, 8 GB RAM and 40 GB+ disk for images and workspaces is a good start.
            li For KVM-accelerated emulators (QEMU) inside a VM, turn on #[strong nested virtualization].
            li No USB or udev setup.

  h3.h6 Preparing an HW machine
  ol.small
    li Install the OS and the Client (#[a(href="#client-setup") Client setup]), then plug in the board's ST-Link, UART adapter and DUT USB.
    li
      | Find each device's USB #[strong port path] (#[code devpath]). It's stable across replugs as long as the device stays in the same port:
      +code('HW machine').
        lsusb -t                                      # USB tree
        udevadm info -a -n /dev/ttyUSB0 | grep -m1 'ATTRS{devpath}'
        st-info --probe                               # ST-Link serials
      | Or open the runner card → #[strong USB devices] → #[strong Refresh], then #[strong Import to config].
    li
      | Put the devpaths into #[code hw-devices] and restart. On every start the Client writes
      | #[code /etc/udev/rules.d/99-thub-&lt;instance&gt;.rules], so the devices appear as #[code /dev/thub/dut&lt;N&gt;-stlink|uart|usb]:
      +code('HW machine').
        sudo systemctl restart thub-client@client
        ls -l /dev/thub/
    li Check the runner card's #[strong Capabilities] tab: each configured device must be present (a missing one is flagged).
    li Your job's #[code --command] flashes the board. The Client never flashes by itself; it passes the ST-Link serials and the downloaded files in the environment (#[a(href="#env") Environment variables]).
  +code('A typical HW job').
    thub run --type hw --label board:nucleo-f401re \
      --download-file https://artifactory.example.com/fw-local/app/1.4.0-42/app.bin \
      --env GH_TOKEN \
      --command 'git clone --depth 1 --branch v1.4.0 "https://x-access-token:$GH_TOKEN@github.com/yourorg/firmware-tests.git" . &&
                 st-flash --serial "$THUB_DUT_STLINK" --reset write "$THUB_DOWNLOAD_1" 0x08000000 && ./ci/test.sh' \
      --wait

  +note('warning').
    #[strong HW in a VM?] USB passthrough works, but the #[code devpath] then reflects the #[em virtual] USB topology and
    can change when the hypervisor reassigns devices. Serial timing-sensitive tests may also suffer. Use a physical machine for HW Clients.

  h3.h6 Preparing an SW machine (real or virtual)
  +code('SW machine / VM').
    sudo apt install -y nodejs npm         # + git, docker.io, … if your jobs' commands use them
    sudo npm i -g @andrian.yablonskyy/thub-client
    nano ~/.config/thub/client.json        # "type": "sw", coordinatorUrl, joinKey
    sudo systemctl enable --now thub-client@client
  p.small.
    To scale out, clone the VM template. In the clone, #[strong before its first start], run
    #[code rm -f ~/var/lib/thub/client/*/.client-id ~/var/lib/thub/client/*.token] and give it its own #[code name].
    Otherwise both VMs register as the same runner.
  +code('A typical SW job').
    thub run --type sw \
      --env GH_TOKEN --env DOCKER_PASSWORD \
      --command 'git clone --depth 1 "https://x-access-token:$GH_TOKEN@github.com/yourorg/firmware-tests.git" . &&
                 export DOCKER_CONFIG="$THUB_WORK_DIR/.docker" &&
                 echo "$DOCKER_PASSWORD" | docker login registry.lab.local:5000 -u ci --password-stdin &&
                 docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/work" -w /work registry.lab.local:5000/dut-emulator:2026.08 make test' --wait

  h3.h6 Checklist for any Client machine
  ul.small.mb-0
    li Correct time (NTP). Timestamps are anchored to the Coordinator's clock, but TLS needs a sane clock.
    li Outbound HTTPS to the Coordinator; test it with #[code curl -sI #{coordinatorUrl}/login].
    li Whatever tools your jobs' commands use (git, docker, a flasher) are installed. Their credentials come with each job as #[code --env] (#[a(href="#git") Using git], #[a(href="#docker") Using Docker]), not from the host.
    li Under systemd, the service can only write to its state directory. Jobs write to #[code $THUB_WORK_DIR], not to #[code ~].
    li Optional: a nightly reboot from the runner card's #[strong Reboot] tab (cron, host-local time). It waits for running jobs.
